1. Critical — a stale token can renew a replacement lease after takeover. In [leases.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/src/services/leases.ts:103), validation and renewal are separate queries. If takeover occurs after line 103, line 104 fetches the newly minted active lease, and lines 106–111 renew it using the old token. Make renewal one atomic conditional update matching `issueId`, `actorId`, `tokenHash`, `invalidatedAt IS NULL`, and `expiresAt > now`; reject unless exactly one row is returned.

2. Critical — expiry can release a lease that was successfully renewed concurrently. [leases.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/src/services/leases.ts:155) selects an expired snapshot, but lines 167–170 later invalidate by ID alone. A heartbeat between those operations can extend `expiresAt`, after which the sweep still invalidates the lease and returns the issue to `todo`. Reassert `expiresAt <= now` and `invalidatedAt IS NULL` in the transactional update, and release the issue only if that conditional invalidation changed one row.

3. Critical — Codex containers receive the environment variable but never send the lease header. [worker-select.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/worker-select.ts:1073) injects `SWITCHYARD_LEASE` for every container engine, but [container-entry.codex.sh](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/container-entry.codex.sh:103) configures only the bearer token. Consequently, every claim-scoped MCP mutation from a Codex container fails even while the host keeps the claim alive. Add an out-of-band lease header mechanism to the Codex MCP configuration and test both engines.

4. Critical — bare-host CLI sessions also never receive the lease. The non-container branch at [agent-worker.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/agent-worker.ts:619) starts `claude` without a lease-bearing MCP configuration or environment integration, while the prompt at line 441 claims writes are automatically authorized. These sessions will fail `update_issue` and `request_human_input`. Either implement hidden header injection for this runner or restrict leased dispatch to the SDK/container paths.

5. High — worker restart adoption drops the heartbeat lifecycle. [adoptContainerSession](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/agent-worker.ts:1180) attaches only `docker wait`; it never recovers the lease token or calls `startLeaseHeartbeat`. After any worker restart, an adopted container’s short lease expires within the remaining heartbeat window and is released while the container continues running. That creates exactly the re-dispatch race the feature is intended to prevent. Restart reconciliation must securely recover the lease and resume heartbeats, or terminate adopted containers before their leases can expire.

6. High — the uptime gate is bypassed by the stale-claim sweep. [webhook-dispatcher.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/src/services/webhook-dispatcher.ts:97) calls `releaseStaleClaims` before gated `expireLeases`. Once a lease’s timestamp has passed, [stale-claims.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/src/services/stale-claims.ts:36) no longer considers it active. Therefore, during the advertised post-restart grace period, any such issue idle for four hours can still be released by the stale sweep. The stale sweep must recognize non-invalidated leases covered by the restart grace, not only currently unexpired leases.

7. High — `setInterval` permits unbounded overlapping heartbeat requests. [agent-worker.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/agent-worker.ts:411) launches a fetch every minute without awaiting or timing out the previous one. A hung tracker accumulates requests indefinitely. Out-of-order completions also corrupt “consecutive” semantics: an older success can reset newer failures, or a backlog of failures can cancel immediately after recovery. Use a self-rescheduling awaited loop with a per-request timeout and abort outstanding work on `stop()`.

8. High — failure before the first successful heartbeat leaves an eight-hour claim. The first request is delayed until the first interval at [agent-worker.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/agent-worker.ts:411). If all ten requests fail, the workload is killed after roughly ten minutes, but the lease was never shortened from its mint TTL. Likewise, synchronous spawn failure at lines 636–638 occurs after the server committed the claim. In both cases the issue remains unavailable for up to eight hours. Perform an immediate heartbeat before/while dispatching and provide a compensating release/invalidation path for dispatch failures.

9. Medium — server configuration can diverge from the hard-coded host timing. The server window is editable at [settings.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/src/services/settings.ts:41), while the host remains fixed at 60 seconds × 10 in [worker-select.ts](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/worker-select.ts:1129). Setting the server window below 600 seconds permits expiry and re-dispatch long before the original worker cancels; setting it above 600 causes premature cancellation. Return heartbeat cadence/window in dispatch policy or otherwise enforce compatible settings.

10. Medium — a committed claim can be orphaned by response loss or malformed JSON. [claimIssueHost](/Users/sean/sites/switchyard/.claude/worktrees/feat+syd-210-claim-leases/scripts/agent-worker.ts:350) may successfully commit the POST but fail at line 356 because the response is lost or invalid. The catch returns `null`, but the issue remains assigned with an unrecoverable plaintext lease for eight hours. Treat claim acquisition as an idempotent operation with a host-generated request/session identifier, or add a safe compensating release protocol.

